## Overview

The rover runs its onboard code automatically on power-up, handling data collection and basic navigation. Once the companion app is connected, you can begin transmitting control signals to the rover.

## Obstacle Avoidance

The rover is equipped with an automatic safety routine that overrides incoming commands when an obstacle is detected. If either the **LIDAR** or **ultrasonic sensor** registers an object within **0.5 meters** of the front of the rover, the following sequence is triggered:

1. The rover stops accepting incoming signals from the app.
2. It reverses for **3 seconds** to create distance from the obstacle.
3. Both sensors log their last recorded distance readings.
4. The onboard camera captures a photo of the detected obstacle.

## Dataset & Classification Model

The data captured during these avoidance events (sensor readings + obstacle images) is intended to train a **classification model** for identifying obstacle types. Due to time constraints, we were unable to implement the model ourselves — instead, we've provided a pre-collected **dataset** that can be used to train one.
